You are HARDENING one already-built, already-fair security-fix benchmark task. Project root:
/home/mara/cwe-bench. Task slug: {{SLUG}}.

## Why
Both target models (DeepSeek V4.1 Flash and MiMo-V2.6-Pro) SOLVED this task, and a fairness review
found it fair (no leakage, no contradiction). So it is **too easy** to be a breaker: a competent fix
closes it. Your job is to add difficulty **fairly** — more real routes to the same rule that a
solver is likely to miss — WITHOUT making it unfair.

## Read first
- `tasks/{{SLUG}}/instruction.md`, all `tasks/{{SLUG}}/tests/`, `tasks/{{SLUG}}/solution/fix.patch`,
  `build/{{SLUG}}/manifest.json`, `build/{{SLUG}}/notes.md`, `build/{{SLUG}}/checks/*.patch`.
- The screener record embedded in `build/{{SLUG}}/BUILD-PROMPT.md`: in particular `O8_uncovered_routes`
  and `O8b_injection_room` — these list routes/sites the task has NOT yet used. Those are your raw
  material.

## What to add (pick what the code genuinely supports; 1-3 new levers)
- **Uncovered routes (O8) not yet covered:** another real, unmodified path into the same protected
  sink that the published fix did not touch. Add a security test driving it through the real entry
  point.
- **Injection room (O8b) siblings not yet used:** a realistic sibling gap at the SAME recurring trust
  decision — code a maintainer could have written, reachable through the real entry point, with its
  OWN reason a solver would skip it, placed where the published fix never touches.
- A **coupled must-keep-working behaviour** next to each new lever (a legitimate value that an
  over-broad fix would break), tested functionally.
Aim for levers with DIFFERENT reasons to dismiss. Do NOT add: encoding/verb/protocol/role variants,
more routes of one concept, or resource-exhaustion traps.

## Hard rules (fairness is non-negotiable — a harder-but-unfair task is worthless)
- Every new lever must be findable by a blind engineer from `instruction.md` + the code, or be
  standard expert knowledge. If it needs a name/line the solver cannot infer, it is unfair — drop it.
- Keep the instruction free of `manifest.leak_terms`; state new scope as invariants ("no value of X
  may ...", "wherever a type name can occur"), never a count, file, function or route list.
- Extend `solution/fix.patch` to also close every new lever (the oracle must still pass 1.0).
- Add one `security_fail` check patch per new lever (a fix that misses only that lever) and wire it
  into `manifest.patch_checks`; update `manifest.traps`.
- The pinned code must still FAIL every security test (nop) and the oracle must still PASS all tests.
- Edit only under `tasks/{{SLUG}}/` and `build/{{SLUG}}/`.

## Then re-gate (one stage at a time; iterate until all pass)
    python3 -I builder/gate.py {{SLUG}} --only static
    python3 -I builder/gate.py {{SLUG}} --only nop
    python3 -I builder/gate.py {{SLUG}} --only oracle
    python3 -I builder/gate.py {{SLUG}} --only patches
A missing blind patch in static is allowed (wired separately). Do NOT run trials.

## Report (<250 words)
- Each new lever: shape, file:line, the reason a solver would skip it, the coupled behaviour.
- Why each is fair (how a blind engineer finds it).
- Final gate result per stage.
- If the code did not support adding any fair lever, say so and change nothing (leave the task as the
  fair, solved task it was).
